Opus encoder: FEC packet-loss floor (default 10%, runtime 0..20%) and
runtime per-call complexity with mid-call change (finding #14), without
touching the fixed 60 ms / 32 kbps CBR config.

- `audio_encoder_opus.cc`/`.h`: new file-local `QaudionMinLossFraction()`
  reads `webrtc::qaudion::OpusMinPacketLossPercent()` (P8; default 10,
  clamped 0..20) and clamps it to the existing `kMaxPacketLossFraction`
  (20%) ceiling. `packet_loss_rate_` - which used to start at a hardcoded
  0.0 and only ever rise once `OnReceivedUplinkPacketLossFraction` got a
  real, smoothed RTCP sample - now starts at this floor, so in-band FEC
  (LBRR) can protect the very first packets of a call. `SetTargetBitrate`'s
  existing bitrate-hysteresis complexity table
  (`GetNewComplexity(config_)`) is now skipped whenever
  `qaudion::OpusEncoderComplexity() >= 0`, so it cannot fight a runtime
  override from the app. `EncodeImpl` polls
  `webrtc::qaudion::TuningGeneration()` once per call (a single atomic
  load, on the encoder's own task queue that already owns `inst_` - no new
  synchronization) and, only when it changed, re-derives the complexity
  (override if set, else the same hysteresis table as before) and
  re-applies the floor on top of whatever the last real loss report was -
  this is the actual "mid-call change" finding #14 asks for. Emits one
  "Q-AUDION opus-enc" log line per applied change (not per frame).
  `api/audio_codecs/opus/audio_encoder_opus_config.h`'s mobile
  `kDefaultComplexity` moves from 5 to 9 (matching the existing non-mobile
  default) - this is the fallback `GetNewComplexity` uses when there is no
  qaudion override and the bitrate is inside the hysteresis window, not the
  day-to-day value once the app calls `setQaudionOpusEncoderComplexity`
  itself.

  Bitrate, ptime and CBR are untouched: nothing here writes
  `config_.bitrate_bps`, `config_.frame_size_ms` or `config_.cbr_enabled`,
  and `WebRtcOpus_SetBitRate`/`WebRtcOpus_EnableCbr` are not called from any
  new code path.

- Finding #14, the other half (see P8's header for the ObjC NSInteger-clamp
  half): `RecreateEncoderInstance()` used to compute `complexity_` from
  `GetNewComplexity(config_).value_or(config_.complexity)` and only read
  `qaudion::TuningGeneration()` afterwards (in an earlier, unshipped sketch
  of this patch) to remember "what generation this complexity_ reflects".
  Reading the generation *after* the value is the actual bug: a concurrent
  `qaudion::SetOpusEncoderComplexity()` landing in the gap would bump the
  generation to a number that already reflects the new value, while
  `complexity_` was still computed from the old one - so `EncodeImpl`'s
  generation check on the next frame would find nothing to do, and the
  update would never be applied until *another* change happened to bump
  the generation again. This patch reads the generation first, then the
  value it corresponds to: the only possible race now is one harmless
  extra re-check on the very next EncodeImpl() call, never a silently lost
  update.

Order: 7th and last of this author's four patches (m150/series), right
after P4b; both depend on P8's qaudion_tuning.h and P4a's compiled-in deep
PLC/OSCE for the self-verification logs to mean anything, though this
patch's own mechanism (FEC floor, encoder complexity) does not itself
require P4a.

Touches (dir "."): modules/audio_coding/codecs/opus/audio_encoder_opus.cc,
modules/audio_coding/codecs/opus/audio_encoder_opus.h,
api/audio_codecs/opus/audio_encoder_opus_config.h.

Verified: `git apply --check` and a real apply are clean against the
pinned webrtc-sdk/webrtc @ ba469aa2093ba950066258ca0a59a6fbd1295582
checkout, on top of P1 + P2 + P3(partial) + P8 + P4b already applied, in
series order. Every symbol/line this patch touches
(`kMaxPacketLossFraction`'s value and location, `packet_loss_rate_`'s
position in the constructor's initializer list, the exact
`RecreateEncoderInstance`/`SetTargetBitrate`/`EncodeImpl` bodies,
`kDefaultComplexity`'s `#if defined(WEBRTC_ANDROID) || defined(WEBRTC_IOS)`
guard) was read from that same pinned checkout, not assumed. Grepped the
resulting tree for RTC_LOG of key/secret/salt/password (none in the
touched files) and for new RTC_CHECK: the two `RTC_CHECK_EQ(0,
WebRtcOpus_SetComplexity(inst_, complexity_))` calls this patch's new code
paths can reach are both pre-existing lines (unchanged), fed either by the
pre-existing, already-bounded `GetNewComplexity` table or by
`qaudion::OpusEncoderComplexity()`, which - like P4b's decoder equivalent -
can only ever be -1 or a value its own setter already validated into
0..10.

Not verified: `clang -fsyntax-only` (same reason as P4b: the local machine
has no toolchain, and setting up enough of the WebRTC header graph on the
build VPS to syntax-check this file would need close to a full gclient
sync).

Security review, stage 3: the floor was only a starting value. The
original SetProjectedPacketLossRate() still clamped to [0, 20 %], so the
first RTCP report with 0 % loss (the normal case on a clean call) dropped
packet_loss_rate_ to 0 and turned LBRR off for the rest of the call, and
EncodeImpl()'s "re-apply the floor" call passed packet_loss_rate_ back to a
function that only acts on a change (a no-op). SetProjectedPacketLossRate()
now clamps to [QaudionMinLossFraction(), 20 %] for every reported value
(RTCP smoother and ANA alike), and EncodeImpl() re-applies it from the last
real loss estimate (the smoother's average, or packet_loss_rate_ under
ANA), so a runtime floor change takes effect in both directions.

